  "worked_solution": "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial:\n\n- The constant term is $30,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3,\\pm 5,\\pm 6,\\pm 10,\\pm 15,\\pm 30.$\n\n- The leading coefficient is $-1,$ and its factors are $\\pm 1.$\n\nFirst, we list out all the possibilities for the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{1}{1},\\text{\u00a0}\\pm \\frac{2}{1},\\text{\u00a0}\\pm \\frac{3}{1},\\text{\u00a0}\\pm \\frac{5}{1},\\text{\u00a0}\\pm \\frac{6}{1},\\text{\u00a0}\\pm \\frac{10}{1},\\text{\u00a0}\\pm \\frac{15}{1},\\text{\u00a0}\\pm \\frac{30}{1}.\n$$\n\nSince the denominators are only $\\pm 1,$ these simplify to:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\text{\u00a0}\\pm 2,\\text{\u00a0}\\pm 3,\\text{\u00a0}\\pm 5,\\text{\u00a0}\\pm 6,\\text{\u00a0}\\pm 10,\\text{\u00a0}\\pm 15,\\text{\u00a0}\\pm 30.\n$$\n\nNotice that none of these potential roots are duplicates. \n\nThis makes a total of $16$ potential roots. (Remember that each $\\pm $ sign accounts for $2$ roots, and $2\\cdot 8=16.$)",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
